Under Swiss federal law, the specific additional integration criteria for the Canton of Zurich are not defined, because federal legislation explicitly delegates this power to the cantons.

The Swiss Citizenship Act sets the federal baseline integration criteria for naturalisation, which require:
- observing public security and order;
- respecting the values of the Federal Constitution;
- communicating in an official language orally and in writing;
- participating in economic life or acquiring education; and
- supporting the integration of family members [1].

For ordinary naturalisation, federal law explicitly authorises the cantons to establish further integration criteria [2]. In addition, procedure in the canton and municipality is regulated by cantonal law [3]. (By contrast, for simplified naturalisation, only the federal integration criteria apply [4]).

Because the additional criteria for Zurich are governed by cantonal legislation rather than federal law, they are not covered by the federal acts and must be checked under cantonal law.

