Under Swiss federal law, you have the right to request information from a controller (such as a company) as to whether personal data concerning you is being processed [1].

### What information you receive
The company must provide the information necessary for you to assert your rights and to ensure transparent processing [2]. In all cases, you are entitled to receive:
- The identity and contact details of the controller;
- The processed personal data as such;
- The purpose of processing;
- The retention period of the personal data (or criteria used to set it);
- Available information on the origin of the data, if not collected directly from you;
- Any recipients or categories of recipients to whom personal data is disclosed; and
- Any automated individual decisions and the underlying logic [2].

Information must be provided free of charge, though exceptions may apply where the effort is disproportionate [3]. As a rule, the information is provided within 30 days [4].

### Does the company have to answer?
Yes, the company is legally required to respond. It may refuse, restrict, or defer access only under specific statutory exceptions [5][6]:
- A formal statute provides for doing so, notably to protect professional secrecy [5];
- Overriding interests of third parties require it [5];
- The request is manifestly unfounded (particularly if it pursues an unlawful purpose) or manifestly vexatious [5]; or
- For a private controller, overriding interests of the controller require it and the data is not disclosed to third parties [6].

Whenever a company refuses, restricts, or defers providing information, it must state the reasons why [7].

