- When the access request is manifestly unfounded or vexatious

In all cases, you are entitled to receive:
- The identity and contact details of the controller;
- The processed personal data as such;
- The purpose of processing;
- The retention period of the personal data (or criteria used to set it);
- Available information on the origin of the data, if not collected directly from you;
- Any recipients or categories of recipients to whom personal data is disclosed; and
- Any automated individual decisions and the underlying logic

It may refuse, restrict, or defer access only under specific statutory exceptions:
- A formal statute provides for doing so, notably to protect professional secrecy;
- Overriding interests of third parties require it;
- The request is manifestly unfounded (particularly if it pursues an unlawful purpose) or manifestly vexatious; or
- For a private controller, overriding interests of the controller require it and the data is not disclosed to third parties.

